  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1267",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Loricaria cataphracta Linnaeus, 1758</li><li>Common names: L062, Cataphract Whiptail</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2020)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Family: Loricariidae. Size: 20 cm. Temperature: 24–28°C. pH: 6.5–7.0. L-number: L062. IUCN: Least Concern (IUCN 2023).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Sensitive to poor water quality especially when first imported and should be acclimatised slowly for the home aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1267",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon River basin and coastal rivers of the Guianas, including the Lower Amazon (Nhamundá), Guyana, and Suriname coastal rivers (Marowijne). Type locality: mouth of the Marowijne River, near Galibi, Suriname.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon River basin, and coastal rivers of the Guianas, Brazil, French Guiana, Guyana, Suriname. Type locality: In America meridionali.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Carnivore, feed with tablets, pellets, insect larvae, zooplankton and shrimps. A varied diet is important to this species and genus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Loricaria species can be differentiated from the similar Rineloricaria species by their feathered sucker-mouths, which the males use for carrying their eggs around after spawning; they are maternal mouthbrooders.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1267",
      "html": "<p>Unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1267",
      "html": "<p>When young, Loricaria spp. can be differentiated from the similar Rineloricaria spp. by their more feathered sucker-mouths. In adults this difference is considerably more evident.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Not the easiest of genera to identify to species; the key for the Loricaria genus is teeth of the upper jaw about twice as long as those of the lower jaw. Abdomen ventrally completely covered by scutes.</p><p>Etymology: The specific name cataphracta is Latin for wearing coats of mail (armour), referring to bony plates on back and sides.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1267",
      "html": "<p>Systema Naturae, Ed. X v. 1, pp 307.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/loricaria_cataphracta.htm",
      "html": "<p>Evers, H.-G. &amp; I.Seidel: Mergus, Baensch Catfish Atlas Volume 1, 1st English edn., 2005. Pp.944. Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628. Salvador, G.N. 2023. Loricaria cataphracta. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2023. The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
